# Expanding the plant catalog

The collection will grow over time (e.g. cherry blossom / flowering cherries). Treat each new plant as a first-class entry, not a one-line mention.

## When the user adds a plant

1. Confirm **botanical name** if possible (common name alone is not enough for care).
2. Check **Melbourne suitability**: winter chill, frost hardiness, summer heat/drought, outdoor vs protected.
3. Assign a **role**: solo specimen, forest candidate, or companion — never mix species in one forest.
4. Cover the same care topics: soil, potting, stem prune, root prune, training, fertilise — with Melbourne months.
5. Note **leaf/flower scale** vs the 60–90 cm target (large leaves or big blooms need a longer refinement plan).
6. Update `index.html` (and any catalog files) so the plant appears in identification + season caveats.

## Melbourne shortlist (examples)

| Interest | Likely species | Notes |
|----------|----------------|-------|
| Cherry blossom | `Prunus × yedoensis`, `P. serrulata`, cold-tolerant flowering cherry | Needs chill; late frost can spoil bloom; deciduous late-winter work |
| Chinese elm | already in collection | Best forest starter |
| English oak | already in collection | Slow; solo |

## Do / don’t

- **Do** say when a species is a poor bonsai fit or needs frost cloth in Melbourne.
- **Do** keep budget soil/pot advice consistent with project rules.
- **Don’t** recommend tropical/indoor-only species without a clear Melbourne caveat.
- **Don’t** put flowering cherry into an elm forest.
